                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "185d7030-a3dc-4ae1-9d82-80d8d9c0264a",
                        "entry_id": "182a057d-1c95-3538-9206-5bbe81ddd305",
                        "tags": [
                            "comparative"
                        ],
                        "form": "more lither"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "188fc210-1c81-48f2-b42b-4d2e6783145f",
                        "entry_id": "182a057d-1c95-3538-9206-5bbe81ddd305",
                        "tags": [
                            "superlative"
                        ],
                        "form": "most lither"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "faf397de-00d6-36b9-932f-5148756c8c4a",
                        "entry_id": "182a057d-1c95-3538-9206-5bbe81ddd305",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Lazy, slothful; listless."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "British",
                            "archaic",
                            "dialectal"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b9e69e9a-e0bc-48d6-9549-820944eeea49",
                                "sense_id": "faf397de-00d6-36b9-932f-5148756c8c4a",
                                "text": "After the same manner a Monk (I mean those lither, idle, lazie Monks) doth not labour and work, as do the Peasant and Artificer: doth not ward and defend the countrey, as doth the man of warre: cureth not the sick and diseased, as the Physician doth: doth neither preach nor teach, as do the Evangelical Doctors and Schoolmasters: doth not import commodities and things necessary for the Commonwealth, as the Merchant doth: therefore is it, that by and of all men they are hooted at, hated and abhorred."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "79d692ad-ab79-43c3-8fd5-370e228c817c",
                                "sense_id": "faf397de-00d6-36b9-932f-5148756c8c4a",
                                "text": "\"It is thine own laziness, thou false English blood, that doest nothing but drink and sleep,\" retorted the page, \"and leaves that lither lad to do the work, that he minds as little as thou.\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "553d8cdf-7c53-4229-9011-d74296180b1b",
                                "sense_id": "faf397de-00d6-36b9-932f-5148756c8c4a",
                                "text": "Secondarily, let him which laboureth in his vocation be prompt and active; let him be watchful and able to abide labour; he must be no lither-back, unapt, or slothful fellow. Whatsoever he doth, that let him do with faith and diligence."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "38d60e73-8779-457f-b677-19359951e955",
                                "sense_id": "faf397de-00d6-36b9-932f-5148756c8c4a",
                                "text": "Thus he [Rabelais] sketched an education which might have befitted a great King, without a word of ribaldry or scorn, and in such a spirit as proves that he gravely condemned the lazy, lither system of the monasteries."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "c7724961-3e5b-3a48-8549-077176567024",
                        "entry_id": "182a057d-1c95-3538-9206-5bbe81ddd305",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Flexible, supple; also, agile, lithe."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "British",
                            "archaic",
                            "dialectal"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9da51234-c1f7-4da3-be9e-fce8701b0720",
                                "sense_id": "c7724961-3e5b-3a48-8549-077176567024",
                                "text": "Thou antique Death, vvhich laugh'ſt vs here to ſcorn, / Anon from thy inſulting Tyrannie, / Coupled in bonds of perpetuitie, / Tvvo Talbots vvinged through the lither Skie, / In thy deſpight ſhall ſcape Mortalitie."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4ed6d6ce-268c-31ba-8dcd-ef9b1fd7ba64",
                        "entry_id": "182a057d-1c95-3538-9206-5bbe81ddd305",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Bad, evil; false."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "a65f9a81-8aa6-4f42-9367-5bc534c53c88",
                                "sense_id": "4ed6d6ce-268c-31ba-8dcd-ef9b1fd7ba64",
                                "text": "The follest slouen ondyr heuen, / Prowde, peuiche, lyddyr, and lewde, / Malapert, medyllar, nothyng well thewde, […]",
                                "translation": "The foullest sloven under heaven, / Proud, peevish, lither, and lewd, / Malapert, meddler, nothing well thewed, […]",
                                "english": "The foullest sloven under heaven, / Proud, peevish, lither, and lewd, / Malapert, meddler, nothing well thewed, […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dd718fc2-b8d8-49eb-9b54-6ab2e649317f",
                                "sense_id": "4ed6d6ce-268c-31ba-8dcd-ef9b1fd7ba64",
                                "text": "For though some be lidder, and list for to rayle, / Yet to lie vpon me they can not preuayle: […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "dac16549-69eb-3d10-8473-6ff90c2cc5f4",
                        "entry_id": "182a057d-1c95-3538-9206-5bbe81ddd305",
                        "glosses": [
                            "In poor physical condition."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "aaad58bb-a1dd-4de3-9c93-c8ea22c02df9",
                                "sense_id": "dac16549-69eb-3d10-8473-6ff90c2cc5f4",
                                "text": "[Y]it lyes / Aphipnas ſnorting faſt a ſléepe not mynding for to wake, / Wrapt in a cloke of Bearſkinnes which in Oſſa mount were take. And in his lither hand he hilld a potte of wyne."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
